value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327772} {:answer/id #uuid "a63e4efb-65ca-582f-aef6-452aaa57060e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{1-{t}^{2}}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328371} {:answer/id #uuid "de35967f-f177-58a6-bb65-1d140d4719fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328878} {:answer/id #uuid "e987c9fc-174b-5d04-89d9-2ba46fe1e8ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328981} {:answer/id #uuid "f30cf7c4-af07-5900-a89d-c8446012d603" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329056}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de35967f-f177-58a6-bb65-1d140d4719fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328878} :answer-field/id #uuid "81231336-19d6-56d7-abf5-f1e9516e7ed8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329397}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "455737fc-ee99-5287-85ce-5747c0a041d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-43855" :question/problem "Let $V=C[-1,0],$ the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[-1,0].$ Find $⟨t,\\sqrt{1-{t}^{2}}⟩$ if the inner product on $V$ is defined as\n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{-1}^{0}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We need to evaluate\n\n$$\n⟨t,\\sqrt{1-{t}^{2}}⟩={\\int }_{-1}^{0}t\\sqrt{1-{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate this integral using the substitution $u=1-{t}^{2},$ $\\text{d}u=-2t\\,\\text{d}t$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}t\\sqrt{1-{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t & =-\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}{u}^{3/2}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}{u}^{3/2}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[-\\frac{1}{3}(1)^{3/2}]-[-\\frac{1}{3}(0)^{3/2}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444592 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133764 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c553cb8-9f5c-5fa5-bc43-db7d7b64755f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Inner Product of Two Vectors in a Vector Space of Continuous Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2397b865-3c09-5174-bc67-a170acce9725" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444552} {:answer/id #uuid "813dae2d-0f53-57fb-9a1a-1d2509827eda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444566} {:answer/id #uuid "97ebceb2-c69d-5984-b715-f21796bdb745" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444569} {:answer/id #uuid "a0cba996-7b68-58e1-ace5-7811f5305eab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444572} {:answer/id #uuid "c7e73e07-ab43-556f-870c-6df1af14fc25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444576}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c7e73e07-ab43-556f-870c-6df1af14fc25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444576} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f0a1a01-415b-5c49-9e24-1991eb5a02c9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444586}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0be188f9-6651-53a5-a98d-3c5cc7f7f4e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-43856" :question/problem "Let $V=C[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}],$ the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$ Find $⟨t,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩$ if the inner product on $V$ is defined as\n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We need to evaluate\n\n$$\n⟨t,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nLet's use integration by parts. We denote\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=t & ⟹ & {u}^{′}=1, \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & ⟹ & v=\\int {v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}t=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting into the formula for the integration by parts, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t & =uv{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2}-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t){|}_{0}^{\\pi /2}-{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡0)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t{|}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡0) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444596 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133762 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e46fa568-0a40-5b36-a01f-20555db9b055" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Inner Product of Two Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "001e21f4-8791-5985-80aa-f25450805342" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444544} {:answer/id #uuid "0f327f02-b0a3-5361-b5e8-983acbd2ecd0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444545} {:answer/id #uuid "1008803b-c2f6-569b-b2ff-1240127da120" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444546} {:answer/id #uuid "19661f33-0f7d-5793-b930-90d7317e07ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444550} {:answer/id #uuid "493843e3-6065-57b3-9ba1-7610a15409bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444557}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "493843e3-6065-57b3-9ba1-7610a15409bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444557} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1dff34c-e9ec-5c79-a9b8-9a73be39c14d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444590}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "81ac8592-2b70-55bf-8038-041515b727a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-76439" :question/problem "An inner product on $V={R}^{3}$ is given by $⟨x,y⟩=3{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+5{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+{x}_{3}{y}_{3}.$ Find $⟨x,y⟩$, if\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,y=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Computing the inner product according to the definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x,y⟩ & =3{x}_{1}{y}_{1}+5{x}_{2}{y}_{2}+{x}_{3}{y}_{3} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 2\\cdot 1+5\\cdot (-1)\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =6-5+9 \\\\  & =10.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444599 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133763 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "efed2bff-625e-556d-99a3-9b51f9d68937" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Inner Product of Two Vectors in a Vector Space of Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1425608e-c991-50c7-b584-e4b9dcc0689e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444548} {:answer/id #uuid "1d6897ab-0bef-54a1-b5ca-789cb37c03a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444551} {:answer/id #uuid "98c7c9b2-31ef-570f-ba9f-9b45433ceeb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444570} {:answer/id #uuid "e30ccf2e-df49-5ce2-b87e-ac6d12abb412" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444578} {:answer/id #uuid "f3d843f2-db61-5940-aedd-063c2d5dca33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444583}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1425608e-c991-50c7-b584-e4b9dcc0689e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444548} :answer-field/id #uuid "64a51fc8-5c03-5f0f-bf50-67aa3a0e0d48" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444587}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b1906542-1837-55c7-bfa3-d76ad056eb3b" :question/math-academy-id "q-76443" :question/problem "Let $V={R}_{2}[t]$ be the vector space of all polynomials of degree at most $2$, equipped with the inner product \n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩=x(-1)y(-1)+x(0)y(0)+x(1)y(1).\n$$\n\n Find $⟨x(t),y(t)⟩$, if\n\n$$\nx(t)={t}^{2}-4,\\,y(t)=3-t.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Computing the inner product according to the definition, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨x(t),y(t)⟩ & =x(-1)y(-1)+x(0)y(0)+x(1)y(1) \\\\  & =(-3)\\cdot 4+(-4)\\cdot 3+(-3)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-12-12-6 \\\\  & =-30.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444593 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133764 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c553cb8-9f5c-5fa5-bc43-db7d7b64755f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Inner Product of Two Vectors in a Vector Space of Continuous Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e0c7b54-4be7-5c97-8ab2-5fe7016d840c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444558} {:answer/id #uuid "c4fcb82a-d6e8-5c8d-b3d3-f18e785cc703" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444575} {:answer/id #uuid "ccf88be4-d19c-5fd7-b5b9-2901367208a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444577} {:answer/id #uuid "f27b3d6e-bdea-597c-bb71-4cb48c36b474" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444581} {:answer/id #uuid "f33dc946-3ee4-50d5-90dd-5604be5bdb3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444582}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f33dc946-3ee4-50d5-90dd-5604be5bdb3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444582} :answer-field/id #uuid "468f980b-08ae-5d3c-bc6e-fd559a6c8692" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444584}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "37beaef3-36bd-59a9-8b05-4fe440be106e" :question/math-academy-id "q-76452" :question/problem "Let $V=C[-\\pi ,0],$ the vector space of all functions that are continuous on $[-\\pi ,0].$ Find $⟨t,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩$ if the inner product on $V$ is defined as\n\n$$\n⟨x(t),y(t)⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{0}x(t)y(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We need to evaluate\n\n$$\n⟨t,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{0}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nLet's use integration by parts. We denote\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=t & ⟹ & {u}^{′}=1, \\\\ {v}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & ⟹ & v=\\int {v}^{′}\\,\\text{d}t=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting into the formula for the integration by parts, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{0}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t & =uv{|}_{-\\pi }^{0}-{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{0}v{u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t){|}_{-\\pi }^{0}+{\\int }_{-\\pi }^{0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(-0\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡0+(-\\pi )\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\pi ))+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{|}_{-\\pi }^{0} \\\\  & =\\pi +(\\operatorname{sin}⁡0-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-\\pi )) \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
